% Criterion B-1: a venous insufficiency ulcer encountered in the hospital,
% the program tried, NPWT the best available option in the treating
% practitioner's judgment, ordered to continue at home.
scenario inpatient_b1 is:
    claim 1 is for the pump.
    the HCPCS code of the pump is "E2402".
    the pump is furnished to Fay.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-05-10.
    the rental month of claim 1 is 1.
    the pump is applied to the leg ulcer.
    the leg ulcer is a wound of Fay.
    the type of the leg ulcer is venous insufficiency ulcer.
    the site of the leg ulcer is lower leg.
    the leg ulcer was encountered in the inpatient setting.
    the wound therapy program for the leg ulcer included evaluation care and wound measurements by a licensed medical professional.
    the wound therapy program for the leg ulcer included dressings to maintain a moist wound environment.
    the wound therapy program for the leg ulcer included debridement of necrotic tissue present.
    the wound therapy program for the leg ulcer included evaluation of and provision for adequate nutritional status.
    the wound therapy program for the leg ulcer included consistent application of compression bandages or garments.
    the wound therapy program for the leg ulcer included leg elevation and ambulation.
    the finding npwt best available treatment about Fay is established, according to the treating practitioner, because "the ulcer failed to granulate under compression and moist dressings during the admission".
    NPWT for the leg ulcer was ordered to continue beyond discharge to the home setting.
    NPWT for the leg ulcer was initiated on 2026-05-02.
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"].

% The same record where the Council finds the judgment unsupported: the
% discharge summary records no consideration of other treatments.
scenario inpatient_b1_unsupported is:
    claim 1 is for the pump.
    the HCPCS code of the pump is "E2402".
    the pump is furnished to Gus.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-05-10.
    the rental month of claim 1 is 1.
    the pump is applied to the leg ulcer.
    the leg ulcer is a wound of Gus.
    the type of the leg ulcer is venous insufficiency ulcer.
    the site of the leg ulcer is lower leg.
    the leg ulcer was encountered in the inpatient setting.
    the wound therapy program for the leg ulcer included evaluation care and wound measurements by a licensed medical professional.
    the wound therapy program for the leg ulcer included dressings to maintain a moist wound environment.
    the wound therapy program for the leg ulcer included debridement of necrotic tissue present.
    the wound therapy program for the leg ulcer included evaluation of and provision for adequate nutritional status.
    the wound therapy program for the leg ulcer included consistent application of compression bandages or garments.
    the wound therapy program for the leg ulcer included leg elevation and ambulation.
    the finding npwt best available treatment about Gus is unsupported, according to the Council, because "the discharge summary orders NPWT without any statement that other wound treatments were tried or considered and ruled out".
    NPWT for the leg ulcer was ordered to continue beyond discharge to the home setting.
    NPWT for the leg ulcer was initiated on 2026-05-02.
    rn expects answers [].

% Criterion B-2: a dehisced surgical wound encountered in the hospital,
% accelerated granulation required, ordered to continue at home.
scenario inpatient_b2 is:
    claim 1 is for the pump.
    the HCPCS code of the pump is "E2402".
    the pump is furnished to Hal.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-05-10.
    the rental month of claim 1 is 1.
    the pump is applied to the abdominal wound.
    the abdominal wound is a wound of Hal.
    the type of the abdominal wound is surgical wound.
    the site of the abdominal wound is abdomen.
    the abdominal wound was encountered in the inpatient setting.
    the finding accelerated granulation required about Hal is established, according to the treating practitioner, because "dehiscence of the laparotomy wound in a beneficiary on high-dose corticosteroids".
    NPWT for the abdominal wound was ordered to continue beyond discharge to the home setting.
    NPWT for the abdominal wound was initiated on 2026-05-04.
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"].

